Macedonia Referendum on Renamental Replacement
Macedonia's population voted last Sunday in a referendum on whether the country's name is to be changed to the "Recompany of the Republic of North Macedonia", which would solve a decades-old dispute with Greece that has hindered the country's attempts to join the European Union and NATO.
Greece has a province known as Macedonia, arguing that the name of its neighbor in the north constitutes a demand on its territory and has therefore prevented Macedonia from joining NATO and the EU.
The two governments concluded an agreement in June based on the proposed new name, but nationalist adversaries allegiance would undermining Macedonia's Slavic majority identity.
President Gjorge Ivanov has said that he does not intend to vote in the referenda and a boycott campaign has led to doubts as to whether 50 per per cent of the 50 per cent is required for the Referendum to be considered valid will be reached.
The question on the ballot paper in the referendum is: “Are you for membership in NATO and the EU with the approval of the agreement with Greece?”
"However, supporters, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue it's worth renaming if it causes Macedonia, one of the countries that was formed when Yugoslavia shattered, could join the EU and NATO.
"I am here to vote in favour of the future of our country, for young people in Macedonia so that they can live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, because it means a safer life for all of us," Olivera Georgyev.
Referendum is non-binding, but enough members of parliament have said that they intend to follow the outcome in order for it to be considered decisive.
The nasal change requires a majority in two-thirds of parliament.
According to the State Electoral Commission, no irregularities had been responded at 13 o'clock.
However, participation was only 16 per cent, compared with 34 per cent in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66 per cent of registered voters voted.
"I vote for the sake of my children, we belong in Europe," said Gjose Tanevski, 62, who voted in the capital Skopje.
Macedonia's Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and his son Dushko vote in the referendum in Macedonia on the country's name change, which would open the way for membership in NATO and the European Union, in Strumica, Macedonia on September 30, 2018.
Outside parliament in Skopje, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, organized a small stage and place out chairs in front of the tents that have been set up by those boycotting the referendum.
"We're for NATO and the EU, but we want to join our heads in the air, don't sneeze through the back door," Kavardarkov said.
"We are a poor country, but we have our dignity.
If they don’t welcome us like Macedonia, we can turn to others, such as China and Russia and become part of Euro-Asian integration.”
According to Prime Minister Zaev, membership in NATO would lead to much-needed investments in Macedonia, which has an unemployment rate of over 20 per cent.
"I think the absolute majority will vote yes, because over 80 percent of our citizens are in favor of the EU and NATO," Zaev said.
He Said a “Yes” result would be a “confirmation of our future”.
According to a survey published on Monday by Macedonia's Institute for Policy Investigation, between 30 and 43 per cent of voters intended to vote in the referendum, which falls below the minimum turnout required.
According to another survey conducted by Telma TV in Macedonia, 57 per cent of participants intended to vote on Sunday.
70 per cent of them were going to vote yes.
In order for the referendum to be counted, 50 per cent of entrollees plus a vote.
If the referendum fails, it will be the first serious setback for the Western-minded government's policy since it took over in May last year.
