Sailors patrol a rug-covered headquarters for the fleet in La Paz.
Public buildings have a sea-blue flag.
Navy bases from Lake Titicaca to the Amazon carry the motto: “The sea belongs to us.
It's our duty to recuperate it.'
In sea-solved Bolivia, the commemoration of the coastline that was lost to Chile in a bloody resource conflict in the 19th-century is still strong – and it’s also the yearning to re-delivery on the Pacific Ocean.
This hope is higher than in several decades now that Bolivia is awaiting a decision by the International Court of Justice on October 1st, after five years of consideration.
"Bolivia has the operation, an ambiguation of unity and peace, and of course awaits the result with a positive attitude," said Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Numerous bolivians will see the international court's decision on large screens the country over, in the hope that the court in The Hague will adjudicate in favor of Bolivia's demands that Chile, after years of unregulated discussions, must negotiate with Bolivia to allow them an outlet of their own to the sea.
Monday's decision also has great importance for Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic native president who faces a controversial battle for re-election next year.
"We are very close to returning to the Pacific Ocean," he pledged at the end of August.
However, some analysts think it's unbelievable that the court judicially judge in Bolivia's favor, and that not much would change even if it does.
U.N. court, based in the Netherlands, does not have the right to award Chilean territory and has stipulated that it will not determine the outcome of possible discussions.
"The fact that the decision is announced only six months after the final arguments suggest the case 'uncompetitive'," said Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert on international law.
And in the last four of the years may rather have harmed than promoted Bolivia's chances.
"Question about access to the sea has been hijacked by the current Bolivian administration," Zárate said.
'She claims Morale's aggressive rhetoric has tarnished Chile's remaining benevolence.
Bolivia and Chile will eventually continue the discussion, but it will be extremely difficult to do so after this.
The two countries have not exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, Bolivia's representative in The Hague, did not consider that the court's decision had been taken unusually quickly.
Monday will give Bolivia "an outstanding opportunity to embark on a new era for the relationship with Chile" and a chance to "put an end to 139 years of disagreements with reciprocal benefits," he said.
Calzadilla also denounced that Morales – still one of Latin America's most popular presidents - used the issue of the sea as a political crutch.
