John Edward: Linguistics Crucial for Global Celebrations
Scotland's independent schools maintain outstanding academic results and this continued in 2018 with yet another set of outstanding test results, strengthened only by individual and collective advances in sports, arts, music and other community engagement.
With up to 30,000 pupil pupils in Scotland the schools, represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), strive to provide the best possible service for their pupils and their parents.
Independent schools strive to prepare their pupils for further education, their chosen career and their role as global citizens.
As an educational sector capable of designing and implementing customized curricula, we see that modern languages remain popular and in-resident subjects at the schools.
Nelson Mandela said: “If you talk to a man in a language he understands it goes to his head.
“If you’re talking to him in his own language, it goes to his heart.”
“It’s a mighty reminder that we don’t just rely on English when we create relationships and trust with people from other countries.
This year's test result shows that languages are at the top of the list, with a maximum number of approved results in independent schools.
A total of 68 per cent of pupils who studied foreign languages were awarded A in the Final Exam Higher Grade.
Data, collected from SCIS 74 member schools, showed that 72 per cent of pupils studying Mandarin received an A in Higher Grade, and 72 per cent of those who studied German, 69 per cent of those who studied French and 63 per cent of those who studied Spanish also received an A.
This shows that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as crucial skills that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Languages such as subject choices are now considered to be just as important as natural sciences, technology, engineering knowledge and maternal at curricula in independent schools and beyond.
A survey conducted by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills in 2014 concluded that, for various reasons, employers had a hard time filling their vacancies, with 17 per cent attributed to a lack of language skills.
Having that being said, language proficiencies become increasingly necessary to prepare young people for their future careers.
With an ever-increasing jobs that require language skills, these competencies become necessary in a globalised world.
Regardless of the career choice, they will have a real advantage in the future if they have embraced a second-language.
Communicating directly with people from other countries automatically gives a competitive advantage.
According to a survey of more than 4,000 adult Britons published by YouGov in 2013, 75 per cent said they couldn't speak a foreign language good enough to hold a conversation in it, and where French was the only language that total came up in a two-digit percentage (15 per cent).
That’s why it’s so important to invest in language tuition for our children.
To be able to speak multiple languages, especially those used in developing countries, gives your child a better chance of finding meaningful employment in the future.
In Scotland, each school will teach different combinations of languages.
Numerous schools will focus on the more classic modern languages, while others will teach languages that are considered most important to the UK for 2020, such as Chinese or Japanese.
Regardless of your child's interest, there will always be a number of languages to choose from in standalone schools, with teachers who are specialists in this area.
Scottish-independent schools strive to provide a learning environment that will prepare children and get them with the skills necessary to succeed, no matter what the future holds.
In a global economic environment, it is undoubtedly language that continues to be vital to the country's future, so it must be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages should really be regarded as "international communication skills."
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence for Scotland's young people.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward Is Head of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
