Ri didn't mention plans for a second summit between Kim and Trump that U.S. leaders elaborated at the United Nations earlier this week.
The minister instead pointed to three rallies between Kim and South Korea's leader Moon Jae-in over the past five months adding: "About the party on this issue of nuclear disarmament has been South Korea and not the United States, the Korean peninsula disarmament wouldn't have come to such an impotence."
Still, the tone of Ri's Speech was dramatically different from last year when he told the United Nations General Assassination that North Korea's future ability to reach the U.S. mainland with rockets was unavoidable after "Mr Evil President" Trump called Kim a "rocket man" on a suicide mission.
This year in the United Nations, Trump, who last year threatened to "completely destroy" North Korea, praise to Kim for his courage to take measures of disarmament, but it said a great deal of work still remains and that sanctions must persist until North Korea disarmed.
On Wednesday, Trump Said He Didn't Have A Timeframe for This and Said "If It Takes Two Years, Three Years or Five Months," doesn't matter."
China and Russia allege that the UN Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the measures taken.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday that “Responsibility of the Security Council’s Sanctions must remain vigulgence and without failure until we realize a complete, final, verified disarmament.”
The Security Council has unsolvated sanctions against North Korea since 2006 in an attempt to stifle the funding of Pyongyang's nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo met in connection with the United Nations General Assortment and then said he would visit Pyongyang again the next month to prepare for a second summit.
