North Korea pledged a meeting with Moon this month to disassemble a missile facility and even a nuclear power complex if the U.S. took "equivalent measures."
'He'd say Kim had told him the 'equivalent measures' he sought were security warranties Trump pledged in Singapore, moving toward the normalization of relations with Washington.
Harvard students take the course on getting enough rest
A new course at Harvard University this year has prompted all doctoral students to improve their sleeping habits in an effort to fight the growing macho culture to study through caffeinated "dailations."
An academic study found that students at the world's leading universities are often clueless when it comes to the very basics of how to take care.
Charles Czeisler, a professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School and a specialist in Brigham and Women's Hospital, designed the course, which he believes is the first of his kind in the United States.
He was inspired to start the course after talking about the impact sleep deprivation had on learning.
"At the end of that a girl came up to me and said, 'Why don't I get to know this until now, during my last year?'
"She said no one had ever told her about the Importance of Sleep - Which Surprised Me," he told The Telegraph.
The course, which was organized for the first time this year, explains to students the importance of how good sleeping habits help academic and athletic performance, as well as improving their general well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at the Harvard Medical School and managing director of the university's health care services, said the university decided to introduce the course after discovering that students suffered from severe sleep deprivation during the week.
The hour-long course includes a series of interactive tasks.
In part of the course there is a picture of a dormming room where students click on coffee cups, curtains, trainers and books to find out about the effects of caffeine and light, how athletic performance is affected by sleep deprivation and the importance of good sleeping habits.
In another part, participants learn how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risks of heart attacks, strokes, depression and cancer.
A map of the campus, with interactive icons, encourages participants to think about their daily routine.
“We know it won’t change students’ behavior right away.
But we think they have the right to know - just as you have the right to know about the health effects of choosing to smoke cigarettes," Professor Czeisler added.
Pride culture that "divaly" still exists, he says and added that modern technology and ever-increasing pressure on pupils meant sleep deficiencies were a growing problem.
Ensuring that you have enough sleep, of good-of-pity, should be the "secret weapon" to fight stress, exhaustion and anxiety, he said - even to avoid weight gain because insomnia puts the brain into starvation mode and makes them constantly hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-Year-Old Califorian who studies chemical and physical biology, helped Professor Czeisler design the course and has read one of his courses last year in his first year at Harvard.
He said the course was an eye-opener and inspired him to campaign for a campus-wide course.
"He hopes the next step is to ask all doctoral students to complete a similar study program before starting their studies at the high-performing institution.
Professor Czeisler recommended pupil pupils contemplate setting an alarm for when to fold and when to waking and being aware of the harmful effects of "blue lights" emitted by electronic screens and LED-finding, which can make you turn around the day, leading to insomnia problems.
