Almanac: Inventor of Geiger Counter
And now a page from Sunday's almanac: 30 September 1882, 136 years ago today ... the day the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method to detect and measure radioactivity, an invention that eventually led to the manick called Geiger meter.
Geiger meters have become a cornerstone of science since then and also figure large in pop culture, as well as in the 1950 film "Bells of Coronado," with the lead role in the seemingly impossible cowpoke scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evans:
Man "What in the world is that?"
Rogers: "It is a Geiger Meter, which is used to locate radioactive minerals, like uranium.
When you put on these headphones, you can actually hear the effects of the atoms emitted by the radioactivity in the minerals. "Evans:
Evans: "Yeah, it's popping now!"
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the invention that bears his name lives on.
New cancer vaccine could teach the immune system to 'see' cancer cells
New Cancer Vaccine Could Teach the Immune System to "See" Cancer Cells and Kill Them
Vaccines Teach Immune System to Recognize Surgery Cells as Part of Treatment
The method involves extracting immune cells from a patient and modifying them in the laboratory
They can then "see" a protein that is common to many cancers and then re-injected
An anti-trial vaccine shows promising results in patients with different cancers.
"A woman treated with the vaccine, which teaches the immune system recognising cancer, saw its ovarian carcinocel cells disappear for over 18 months.
The method involves extracting immune cells from a patient and modifying them in the laboratory so they can "see" a protein that is common to many cancers known as HER2, and then re-injection the cells.
Professor Jay Berzofsky, from the US National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland, said: "Our findings suggest we have a very promising vaccine."
HER "promotes the growth of several types of cancer, including breast, ovarian, pulmonary and large intestine carcinoma, explained Prof Berzofsky.
A similar method of taking immunotal cells from patients and "teaching" them how targeting cancer cells has worked in the treatment of a type of leukaemia.
