HIV/Aids: China Reports 14% Increas
China has announced an increase of 14% in the number of citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
More than 820 000 people have been affected in the country, the health authorities said.
Approximately 40 000 new cases were only reported in the second quarter of 2018.
The vast majority of new cases were transmitted via sex and marked a change from previous years.
Traditionally, HIV spreads rapidly through certain parts of China as a result of infected blood transfusions.
"But the number of people infected with HIV in this way had been reduced to almost zero, Chinese health authorities said at a conference in Yunnan province.
However, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China has risen by 100 000 people year-on-year.
HIV-infection through sexual intercourse is an acute issue in China's LGBT community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is said to be widespread.
Due to the country's conservative values, studies have estimated that 70-90% of men who have sex with men sooner or later will marry women.
Numerous seizures are due to insufficient protection when interconnected in these relationships.
China's government has since 2003 pledged universal access to HIV medicine as part of the efforts to address the problem.
