What is the Global Citizen's Festival and hasn't it done to reduce poverty?
This Saturday New York will host the Global Citizen Festival, an annual music event that has a hugely impressive line-up of stars appearing and an equally impressive mission; fighting world poverty.
Now in its seventh year, the Global Citizen Festival will see tens of Thousands of People pilgrimage to Central Park's Great Lawn, not only to enjoy performers like Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to raise awareness of the event's real-world goal: to fight extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen's Festival is, according to a statement from 2012, extending the Global Poverty Project, an international anti-language group that hopes to combat poverty by increasing the number of people actively battling it.
In order to get a free ticket to the event (if you weren't ready to pay for a VIP ticket), concert guests would perform a series of tasks, or "measures" such as volunteering, e-mail a world leader, call or in other meaningful ways help raise awareness of the goal of ending poverty.
But how successfully has Global Citizen been 12 years left to achieve its goal?
Is the idea to reward people with a free concert a genuine way to persuade people to act, or is it just about so-called "clicktivism" - people who feel they make a real difference by signing an online filament or typing a tweet?
Since 2011, Global Citizen has registered more than 19 million "documents" from its adherents who have fought for a variety of goals.
It states that these actions have helped spur world leaders to announce commitments and policies equivalent to more than $37bn, which will affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
In the beginning of 2018, the group quoted 390 commitments and announcements stemming from its actions, including at least $10bn has already been disbursed or funded.
The group estimates that so far the assets collected have had a direct impact on nearly 649 million people worldwide.
Some of the most important commitments are The Power of Nutrition, a partnership based in the United Kingdom between investors and players involved in "helping children develop to their full potential," promising to give Rwanda $35 million to help combat malnutrient in the area after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from global citizens.
"With the support of the British government, donors, national governments and global citizens, just like you, we can make social injustice as malnutrient is a footnote in history," the ambassador for partnership Tracey Ullman told the crowd during a live concert in London in April 2018.
"The group also stated that after more than 5000 measures taken requiring the United Kingdom to improve the diet for mothers and children, the government announced the funding of the project, the Power of Nutrition, encompassing dietary programs aimed at 5 million women and children.
In response to one of the usual questions on its website: "what makes you think we can fight extreme poverty?"
Answered the Global citizen: "It's going to be a long and dire way - sometimes we're going to fall and fail.
However, as the great civil rights and anti-apartheid movements before us, we will succeed, because we are stronger together.
Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are some of the performers performing at this year's New York, whose hosts are Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
