Inventor of the World Wide Web plans to start a new internet to compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, launches a startup that aims to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
Technology legend's latest project, Inrupt, is a company based on Berners-Lee's open source platform Solid.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is stored and which people can access what information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee jodded that the intention behind Inrupt is "World Dominance."
"We've got to do it now," he said about the startup company.
"It's a historic moment."
The app uses Solid's technology to allow people to create their own "staff online data store" or a POD.
It may include contact lists, making-lists, calendar, music libraries and other personal and professional tools.
It's as if Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all available in a web browser at the same time.
What's unique about the personalized online data storage is that it's entirely up to the user who can access what kind of information.
The company calls it "personal empowerment through data."
The idea with Inrupt, according to the company's CEO John Bruce, is that the company should include resources, processes and appropriate skills to make Solid available to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by IBM, some software developers who will work with the project and a group of volunteer codivers.
Beginning with this week, technology developers around the world can create their own decentralized apps using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee Said he and his team aren't talking to "Facebook and Google about whether to introduce a complete change where all the business models are set on high edge overnight.
"We don't ask them for permission."
In a post on the Medium published on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that Inrupt's mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem to protect the integrity and quality of the new web created by Solid.
In 1994 the Berners-Lee transformed the Internet when he established the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
Berners-Lee has in recent months been an influential voice in the debate on net neutrality.
Also during the launch of Inrupt, Berners-Lee will continue to be the Founder and Director of the World Wide Web Consortium, Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm very optimistic about this new era on the web," Berners-Lee added.
