The only Priest in the Church of England who was rewarded with a Victoria cross during the First World War is now celebrated in his hometown 100 years later.
Lt Col Reverend Bernard Vann received the award for his actions during the attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt on September 29, 1918.
However, he was slaughtered by a sniper four days later and was never told that he had been awarded the British military's most prestigious prize.
A memorial stone was unveiled by his two granddaughters during a parade in Rushden, Northampton, on Saturday.
One of his granddaughters, Michael Vann, said it was "brilliant symbolically," that the stone should be revealed exactly 100 years after his grandfather was rewarded with the award.
