Germany's new warship postponed again
The German navy's newest frigate would have been launched in 2014 to reimburse warships of the Cold War, but it won't launch until at least next year due to inaccurate systems and high costs, local media reported.
Incorporation of "Rheinland-Pfalz," the lead ship for the all-new Baden-Wuerttemberg frigates, has now been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to the Die Zeit newspaper quoting a military spokesman.
The ship should have joined the navy as early as in 2014, but the after-delivery problems arose were fatal for the ambitious project.
The four Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ships commissioned in 2007 will reimburse the aggravating Bremen frigates.
They will feature a vigorous cannon, a string of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as some stealthy techniques, such as reduced radar, infrared and acoustic signature.
Other important functions include longer maintenance periods - it should be possible to station the newest frigates for up to two years without contact with home ports.
But Continuing delays mean the cutting-edge war ships - which allow Germany to project power abroad - are already obsessive when they're put into operation," Die Zeit wrote.
The dire F125 frigate made headlines last year, when the German navy officially refusing to put the ship into operation and returned to Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
This was for the first time the navy returned a ship to a shipbuilder after delivery.
Little was known about the causes of the remnant, but German media indicated a number of important "software and hardware flaws" which made the warship useless if it was used on a combat mission.
Deficiencies in the software were particularly important as ships in the Baden-Wuerttemberg class will be driven by a staffing of about 120 sailors - only half the crew of older frigates in the Bremen-Class.
It also emerged that the ship has a significant excess weight, leading to impaired performance and limiting the navy's ability to carry out future upgrades.
The 7,000-three-grey "Rheinland-Pfalz" is presumed to be dual as monotonous vessels used by the Gentlemen during World War II.
Aside from inaccurate hardware, even the entire project's price tag - including the training of the crew - becomes a problem.
It is said to have amounted to staggering $3.1 billion ($3.6 billion) - compared to €2.2-billion-in-earth.
Difficult problems affecting the latest Fridays become particularly important in the light of recent warnings that Germany's naval power is declining.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels acknowledged, the head of the German Parliament's defence committee, that the navy actually "begins to have a shortage of fully-equipped ships."
"The official said it is a growing problem, because old ships are decommissioned but no replacement ships were provided.
He deplored that none of the Fridays in the Baden-Wuerttemberg class could join the Navy.
The National Trust eavesdrops to bats' secret life
New research carried out on a real estate in the Scottish Highlands aims to reveal how bats use the landscape in their pursuit of food.
We hope that the results will shed new light on the behavior of unique flying mammals and help guide future conservation activities.
The study, carried out by researchers at the National Trust for Scotland, will follow south and dwarf fispistral as well-browed and Daubenton flatter mice at Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Particular recorders will be placed in key locations around the property to track bat activities throughout the season.
NHS personnel and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using hand-held detectors.
Expert audio analysis of all recordings will determine the frequency of flutter mouse sounds and which species do what.
A habitat map and report will then be produced to create a detailed area image of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, nature conservation adviser for NTS, hope the results will reveal which areas of habitat are most important for the bats and how they are used by each of the species.
This information will help to determine the benefits of habitat-management work such as meadow-poison creation and how best to maintain forest lands for bats and other related species.
Bat populations in Scotland and in the United Kingdom have declined significantly over the past century.
They are threatened by construction and development work that affect nests and habitat loss.
Wind turbines and lighting can also pose a risk, such as aircraft and some chemical treatments of building materials as well as attacks from house cats.
Bats aren’t blind.
But because of their nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are more useful than their eyes when it comes to capturing precipitates.
They use a sophisticated eco-localization technology to find insects and hurdles in their airway.
NTS, which is responsible for the care of more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land around the country, takes bats very seriously.
The organization has ten-educated experts, who regularly carry out examinations, scrutinize nests and sometimes carry out rescue operations.
The organization has also established Scotland's first and only dedicated bat reserve on the Threave Estate in Dumfries and Galloway, home for eight of Scotland's ten bat species.
Property manager David Thompson says the property is the ideal area for them.
"Here at Threave we have a good area for bats," he said.
"We've got the old buildings, lots of veteran trees and a variety of beneficial habitats.
"But there's a lot about bats that are still unfamiliar, so the work we're doing here and on other properties helps us understand more about what they need to thrive."
He emphasizes the importance of searching for bats before carrying out maintenance in real estate, as it is possible that unintentional destruction of a single nest could kill up to 400 shepherds and young, and possibly wipe out an entire population.
Bats are protected and it is illegal to kill, bothernate or disturb them or destroy their nests.
Elisabeth Ferrell, the Scottish officer of the Bat Conservation Trust, has encouraged the public to help.
She said: "We still have a lot to learn about our bats and for many of our species we don't know how their populations are doing."
