"The statements from not just Toyota but other manufacturers are that we absolutely need to be able to continue what has been a very successful set of supply chains."
Toyota couldn't say how long production would be halted, but in the longer term warned that increased costs would reduce the plant's competitiveness and eventually cost jobs.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has worked in Burnaston for 24 years and is the leader of the Unite union at the factory, said his members are increasingly worried: "My experience is that these jobs will never come back.
