Then he helped Alloa come back from relegation to the third division, lifting St Mirren from being close relegation to becoming the winner of the championship title the following season.
Ross says he's more at ease now than ever during his playing career in Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
"It was probably a crossroads," he recalls, referring to when he enrolled the command of Alloa.
“I really thought that leadership was just right for me, in a completely different way than playing.
"It sounds strange, because I had okay results and could live decently on it. In addition, I had some great successes.
But playing can be tough.
There is a lot you have to get through every week.
It's still the stress and pressure at work for me, but exercising feels just right.
"I've always wanted to train and now I do. I'm feeling better than I've ever done throughout my adult life."
