A company that sells breast pumps has caused disagreements on the internet with an ad that compares breastfeeding mothers with cows being milked.
To draw attention to the launch of what is said to be “the world’s first silent portable breast pump”, the consumer tech company Elvie has released a music video-in-piroined commercial with a humorous appropriation to show what freedom the new pump gives mothers pumping breast milk.
Four real-life mothers dance in a haught-filled barn with cows to a lyric lines like: “Yeah, I’m milking myself, but don’t have a tail” and “If you didn’t know it, this isn’t a udder, but tits.”
The chorus continues: “Pump out, pump out, I feed the babies, pump out, pump out, I’m milking the ladies.”
However, the commercial, which has been posted on the company's Facebook page, has wreaked on the internet.
The film has received 77,000 views and hundreds of remarks, and has faced mixed reactions from the viewers. A lot of people say that it sheds light on the dairy industry “phases”.
“A very poor decision to use cows to promote this product.
Just like us, they have to get pregnant and give birth in order to produce milk, but their cubs are taken away from them just a few days after the birth,” one person wrote.
