In 2004: A major earthquake at the west coast of Indonesian Aceh province on northern Sumatra on 26 December triggered a tsunami affecting 14 countries. 226,000 people died along the coasts of the Indian Ocean. More than half of them were in Aceh.
2005: A series of powerful earthquakes hit Sumatra's west coast in late March and early April.
Hundreds of people died on the island of Nias, off Sumatra's coast.
IN 2006: A magnitude 6.8 on the Richter Scale struck south of Java, Indonesia's most populpied island, triggering a sncent tsunami slapping over the southern coast, killing nearly 700 people.
2009: A 7.6 demarcation embossed near the city of Padang, the capital of the province of Western Sumatra.
More than 1,100 people died.
IN 2010: A magnitude 7.5 earthquake hit one of the Mentawai Islands outside Sumatra. A tsunami of up to ten metres was triggered. Dozens of villages were destroyed and around 300 people were killed.
2016: A root-early earthquake struck the Pidie Jaya in Aceh management area and caused destruction and panic, when people were reminiscent of devastation at the deadly quake and tsunami in 2004.
At this time no tsunami was triggered, but more than 100 people were killed by buildings falling apart.
2018: Large earthquakes affected the Indonesian tourist island of Lombok. More than 500 people died, most on the northern side of the island.
