Why U.S.-China Trade War Could Be Good for Beijing
The opening tier of the U.S.-China trade was desolate. The war is far from over, but the rift between the countries could benefit the Chinese government in the long term, according to experts.
U.S. President Donald Trump was firing the first warning shot earlier this year by ouming key Chinese export supplies such as solar panels, steel and aluminum with tariffs.
The most significant escalation arrived this week, with new tariffs on $200 billion goods. In effect, this means that half of all the goods entering the United States from China are duty-procured.
Beijing has each time responded with the same coin. Recently it threw away charges of between five and ten per cent on American goods for $60bn.
China has pledged to match U.S. actions. The world's second-largest economy probably won't de-escalate in foreseeable times.
Getting Washington to back down means bowing to demands, but publicly crawling for the United States would be far too embarrassed for Chinese President Xi Jinping.
"However, experts argue that if Beijing plays its cards right, the pressure from the U.S. trade war could bring positive support to China in the long term, by reducing the interdependence of the two economies.
"A quick political decision in either Washington or Beijing can create the conditions for economic panic in either country, and it's actually significantly more permeable than viewers in the past have wanted to admit," said Abigail Grace, a research assistant with a focus on Asia at the think tank Center for New American Security.
Syria 'poonly' for returning refugees, according to the foreign minister
Syria says the country is ready to receive voluntarily returning refugees and pleals for help to rebuild the country, located in ruins after the more than seven-year-around war.
In a Speech Before the United Nations General Assemblies, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem said the conditions of the country are now improving.
"The situation in place today is more stable and secure, thanks to our success in fighting terrorism," he said.
The government continues to redevelop the areas that have been devastated by terrorists, to re-emergency.
There are now all the preconditions for the voluntary resuscitation of refugees to the country they were forced to leave because of terrorism and unilateral economic measures directed against their daily lives and subsistence.
The U.N. estimates that more than 5.5-million Syrians have relocated the country since the war began in 2011.
Another six-millions of people still living in the country are in need of humanitarian support.
According to Al-Moualem, the Syrian regime would welcome help to redefeate the destroyed country.
However, he emphasized that it does not receive conditional support or assistance from countries that have supported the insurgency.
