Algae blosciation: Halts are declining in Pinella, Manate and Sarasota
The latest report from the Florida Fisheries and Wildlife Commission shows a general decrease in algae bloscieration concentrations for areas around Tampa Bay.
According to the Commission, unequilied flourishes are being reported in the areas around large municipalities Pinella, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier.
The algae blosciation stretches along just over 20 miles of the coast, from northern Pinella's to southern Lee.
Stains can be seen about one- and a-a-half miles out into the sea outside the main municipality of Hillsborough, but in fewer places compared to last week.
Algal blosing has also been observed in the large municipality of Pasco.
Medium concentrations in or off the coast of Pinellas have been accounted for over the past few weeks. Outside Hillsborough coast low to high concentrations, background concentrations to high concentrations in Manatee, background concentrations to high concentrations inside or off the coast in Sarasota, background concentrations to medium or off-the-coast in Lee, and low concentrations in Collier.
Rehabilitation reports continue to come from major municipalities Pinella, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
There were no reports of reconnaissance rhettriation in north-western Florida this past week.
